ges a day and  something that's kind of hard but not  too hard and you think it's going to be  fun and it might have nothing to do with  how that industry works but on the other  hand if you can get the intelligence  maybe by by interning with someone who  can actually teach you about the field  you really care about and you understand  this is what's involved then you know  where to put your energy rule number  eight explore let's say you had a time  machine and you could go back to meet a  young Steve Jobs I probably the first  thing you would do is go buy Apple stock  but let's say you go back even earlier  than that in a high school age Steve  Jobs the biographical sources we have  today suggest that if you talk to a  young Steve Jobs you would not come away  with the idea that he was passionate  about building a technology company did  not have at that point in his life a  passion for changing the world through  technology right so Steve Jobs did not  go to Berkeley to study Electrical  Engineering which is what she would have  done in that time in that place if you  were really passionate about electronics  and he didn't go to Stanford or UCLA to  study business which is probably what  you would have done in that time in that  place  if you're very passionate  entrepreneurship and business he instead  went to Reed College a liberal arts  school up in Oregon you studied history  study dance pretty soon afterwards he  dropped out and hung out on campus  walking around barefoot experimenting  with sort of extreme diets bumming free  meals from the Hare Krishna temple  eventually he got fed up with being  completely destitute came back to  California took a night shift job at  Atari and that was very specific because  he wanted flexibility and not too much  responsibility and he began the study  Eastern mysticism way more seriously  that it just arrived on the shores of  the west coast in this period he went on  a min deqin journey to India in this  point months-l